          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-825337cf31d56b81\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Sonya directly avows that she loves Natasha. The utterance primarily presents her affection as true, helping deny envy and affirm gratitude. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and although delivered while Sonya is distressed and crying, the words occur within a deliberate explanation after she has gathered her strength; there is no independent evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Sonya addresses Natasha in direct dialogue: «я тебя люблю» (“I love you”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Sonya contrasts Natasha’s unobstructed prospects with Boris against her own threatened relationship with Nikolai. Her avowal reassures Natasha that she is not envious and belongs to a broader declaration of love and gratitude toward the Rostov household. Her tears and emotional intensity concern her own predicament, but do not transform this considered avowal into an expressive/reflexive utterance or a relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Тебе хорошо... я не завидую... я тебя люблю, и Бориса тоже» (“It’s all right for you… I’m not envious… I love you, and Boris too”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force; its immediate function is also to deny jealousy toward Natasha and Boris.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya speaks «собравшись немного с силами» (“having gathered a little strength”) while explaining the obstacles to marrying Nikolai.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is part of an effortful, coherent explanation rather than positively marked as an involuntary or reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya continues: «я так люблю и ее, и всех вас» and says she is so grateful that she would gladly sacrifice everything.\",\"supports\":\"The continuation corroborates the avowed affection and gratitude, while the separate hypothetical sacrifice does not make the highlighted core utterance itself a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as an affirmative, canonical-present, explicit Russian love construction and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted phrase is an ordinary explicit first-person avowal directed to a second-person object.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Sonya’s emotional distress, reassurance, and surrounding gratitude are adequately represented as context without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
